# What your app does when one of its customers' QuickBooks Desktop connections changes. The
# qube_rails gem does the rest: it checks each event is this connection's and newer than the
# last one applied, under the connection row's lock, and updates the row's status
# ("connected", "setup_cancelled", "stalled") and stall_reason before your handler runs.
#
# connection is the QubeRails::Connection (one company file; an account can have several) and
# owner the account that connected it. Start a run on it with
# QuickbooksDesktop::<Operation>.start(record, connection).
module QuickbooksDesktop
  class ConnectionEvents < QubeRails::ConnectionEvents
    # QuickBooks is connected: show it, and start syncing.
    def connected(event)
      log_event(event)
    end

    # They chose "Cancel setup" on the setup page: offer to set it up again
    # (POST /quickbooks/connections/<id>/onboarding).
    def setup_cancelled(event)
      log_event(event)
    end

    # Your requests are waiting and QuickBooks hasn't been reached. Show the customer, and tell
    # them what connection.stall_reason says:
    # - "not_checking_in": the Web Connector isn't calling in. Turn on the QuickBooks computer,
    #   and check the Web Connector runs.
    # - "quickbooks_unavailable": it is calling in and can't reach QuickBooks. Open QuickBooks
    #   with the company file.
    # A stall can send this again with a new reason: show the newest. resumed clears it.
    def stalled(event)
      log_event(event)
    end

    # Clear what stalled showed, whichever its reason.
    def resumed(event)
      log_event(event)
    end
  end
end
